1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,963
This is a 1960 Willys DJ-3A Surrey: tiny, open-air, and unapologetically pink.

2
00:00:06,963 --> 00:00:10,566
Earlier, before this Build Week extension, Beachcar surfaced it.

3
00:00:10,566 --> 00:00:13,276
I didn't know to search for it.

4
00:00:13,276 --> 00:00:16,464
I bought it. That's the clearest proof.

5
00:00:16,664 --> 00:00:19,500
Alerts can match make, year, and price.

6
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:23,646
My taste is harder: joyful, mechanically simple, useful for slow beach days,

7
00:00:23,646 --> 00:00:25,323
charming instead of precious.

8
00:00:25,323 --> 00:00:29,438
Beachcar turns that preference into an agent that can act on it.

9
00:00:29,638 --> 00:00:32,239
Beachcar already had an iPhone review experience.

10
00:00:32,239 --> 00:00:36,799
For Build Week, I extended it with taste coaching and a desktop handoff.

11
00:00:36,799 --> 00:00:39,675
Local Codex connects to Beachcar's hosted MCP,

12
00:00:39,675 --> 00:00:43,562
where taste, feedback, sources, and published runs become durable memory.

13
00:00:43,762 --> 00:00:49,207
Inside the product, GPT-5.6 is the judgment engine.

14
00:00:49,207 --> 00:00:54,032
Running in your own Codex, it reads the brief, plans the search,

15
00:00:54,032 --> 00:00:56,883
uses your signed-in browser, compares auction evidence,

16
00:00:56,883 --> 00:01:00,428
and explains fit, value, uncertainty, and risk.

17
00:01:00,628 --> 00:01:03,603
This deterministic replay makes the contract inspectable.

18
00:01:03,603 --> 00:01:07,383
One fixture carries the same run identifier through setup, research,

19
00:01:07,383 --> 00:01:10,288
publication, verification, and the review state.

20
00:01:10,288 --> 00:01:13,578
Separately, a golden-path smoke test publishes and reads back

21
00:01:13,578 --> 00:01:17,254
a synthetic run through the hosted MCP.

22
00:01:17,254 --> 00:01:21,874
In actual use, local Codex, the signed-in browser, and MCP

23
00:01:21,874 --> 00:01:25,549
are designed to add zero Beachcar AI usage.

24
00:01:25,549 --> 00:01:27,124
Credentials stay local.

25
00:01:27,124 --> 00:01:30,799
OpenAI costs stay with the user's Codex.

26
00:01:30,999 --> 00:01:32,908
The goal isn't another feed.

27
00:01:32,908 --> 00:01:34,787
The replay carries five fixture cars;

28
00:01:34,787 --> 00:01:38,692
each candidate has a reason, caveat, and source in the contract.

29
00:01:38,692 --> 00:01:41,187
The real Surrey proves the taste problem.

30
00:01:41,187 --> 00:01:44,975
This local path reasons from character and context, not just keywords.

31
00:01:45,175 --> 00:01:47,375
Then the loop gets personal.

32
00:01:47,375 --> 00:01:49,881
Love the Surrey, and tomorrow's plan prioritizes

33
00:01:49,881 --> 00:01:53,029
open-air oddballs, playful color, and slow-car charm.

34
00:01:53,029 --> 00:01:56,551
Mark an anonymous performance SUV as “Never again,”

35
00:01:56,551 --> 00:01:59,090
and Codex stops spending attention there.

36
00:01:59,090 --> 00:02:02,442
Feedback becomes inspectable memory, not a hidden profile.

37
00:02:02,642 --> 00:02:08,532
I used Codex with GPT-5.6 to build this Build Week extension.

38
00:02:08,532 --> 00:02:12,167
It audited the repository, helped choose an honest local-agent architecture,

39
00:02:12,167 --> 00:02:15,667
implemented iPhone-to-desktop onboarding and the scoped MCP workflow,

40
00:02:15,667 --> 00:02:18,494
drove the browser and iOS Simulator,

41
00:02:18,494 --> 00:02:20,682
and strengthened the golden-path tests.

42
00:02:20,682 --> 00:02:22,870
I made the product decisions.

43
00:02:22,870 --> 00:02:25,394
Codex turned them into tested software.

44
00:02:25,594 --> 00:02:29,587
Beachcar is for Apps for Your Life because it measures success differently.

45
00:02:29,587 --> 00:02:32,042
Not more listings, clicks, or engagement.

46
00:02:32,042 --> 00:02:33,669
One unusually good decision.

47
00:02:33,669 --> 00:02:36,775
Your Codex. Your taste. The one.
